import{SRC,range,f,sum,universal,benOr,benOrTrials,omegaTrace,ballots,sperner,approximate,diamond,panel,grid}from'./ch05.shared.js';
export default{collection:'dist',chapter:5,items:[
{
num:'5.9',kind:'Theorem',name:'Universality of consensus',
statement:String.raw`For a fixed finite number n of asynchronous processes, any deterministic sequential object with total terminating operations has a linearizable wait-free implementation from atomic read/write registers and an unbounded supply of n-process consensus objects. The construction orders invocations in a common linked log, computes the specified sequential transitions, and helps announced pending operations. It assumes atomic operations of the supplied consensus objects, rather than implementing them from registers.`,
idea:String.raw`Each log cell has a consensus object choosing its successor. An operation announces a fresh cell, finds a sufficiently recent head, and proposes either its own cell or the pending cell selected cyclically by the current sequence number. Competing processes therefore attach one common successor; applying the deterministic operation to the predecessor state yields a common result. Cyclic helping bounds how far a live operation can be bypassed: Herlihy's construction threads it within at most n+1 main-loop iterations after its head scan.`,
caption:String.raw`Schedule actual steps of a helping construction for an integer accumulator. Cells contain increments, sequence numbers and the exact resulting state; all processes announce before scheduling. A halted process's operation may still be helped into the shared log.`,
deps:['dist-5.8','dist-4.8'],refs:['Herlihy1991 §4, Figure14, Theorem14, pp143–146'],links:{source:SRC.herlihy},tags:['universality','linearizability','helping'],scope:String.raw`Authored finite execution of the fresh-cell construction for3…5 processes, one operation per process, increment p+1, initial state0. One displayed step groups atomic successor consensus, deterministic state calculation and head update; no memory reclamation or finite-memory bound is claimed. General operations and concurrent invocation timing are covered by the theorem, not by this one-shot experiment.`,
viz(v){const n=v.slider('process count',3,5,3,{int:true}),halt=v.select('halt after announcement',['none','P0','P1','P2'],'P1'),seed=v.slider('schedule seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),steps=v.slider('helping iterations',0,20,4,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'linearized accumulator log'),g=panel(v,'threaded announcements');let A;v.render(()=>{A=universal(n.value,seed.value,steps.value,halt.value==='none'?-1:+halt.value.slice(1));s.setView([-.5,n.value+.6],[-1,sum(range(n.value).map(i=>i+1))+3]);s.dot(0,0,{r:5,color:'muted'});let previous=[0,0];A.log.forEach((c,j)=>{s.path([previous,[j+1,c.state]],{color:'blue',width:2});s.dot(j+1,c.state,{r:6,color:'blue'});s.text('P'+c.p,j+1,c.state+1,{size:11,align:'center'});previous=[j+1,c.state];});grid(g,A.announce.map((id,p)=>['+'+(p+1),A.cells[id].seq||'pending',A.iterations[p]]));});v.legend([['blue','log prefix / cumulative state'],['purple','columns: increment / sequence / own iterations']]);v.readout(()=>`Log order ${A.log.map(x=>'P'+x.p).join(' → ')||'empty'}; accumulator ${A.log.at(-1)?.state??0}. Threaded ${A.log.length}/${n.value}; own loop counts ${A.iterations.join(', ')}. Every cell's state equals predecessor state plus its invocation. All invocations overlap here; log order is a legal linearization. Helping a halted invocation is permitted.`);}
},
{
num:'5.10',kind:'Algorithm',name:'Ben-Or randomized consensus',
statement:String.raw`Ben-Or's crash-consensus protocol uses reliable asynchronous messages and private random bits with n>2t. Each round gathers n−t first-phase values; a strict majority of all n senders permits a second-phase ratification. Gather n−t second-phase messages: adopt any ratified value, decide it if more than t ratify, and otherwise flip a fresh fair bit only when none ratify. Processes keep participating after deciding. Under fair delivery and a weak adversary unable to observe message contents, ideal independent coins give termination with probability1; safety is deterministic.`,
idea:String.raw`Two different values cannot both obtain a first-phase strict majority. If someone sees t+1 ratifications and decides v, every other second-phase quorum meets that ratifier set, so all correct processes adopt v and decide it in the next round. When no decision has occurred, there remains positive conditional probability that the undecided preferences align; the weak-adversary termination argument repeats such opportunities. Finite seeded trials neither establish probability1 nor implement mathematically independent ideal coins.`,
caption:String.raw`Run the actual two-phase message protocol with a fair FIFO or seeded delivery order. Inspect decisions, round progress and a distribution of32 separately seeded runs. Budget-exhausted runs are right-censored, not labeled nonterminating.`,
deps:['dist-5.7'],refs:['Aspnes2003 randomized consensus survey §5.1, Algorithm1, pp9–11; inspected crash variant, not original Ben-Or body'],links:{source:SRC.benor},tags:['randomized consensus','private coins'],scope:String.raw`Five processes, t1 or2, at most one initial crash. Each sender contributes once per phase/round; future-phase messages are buffered. Oldest queued message is delivered every fifth event, preventing starvation; seeded scheduling and coin streams are separate LCGs; scheduling does not inspect payloads or coin outcomes. Trial cap1000 deliveries; clock-free message-step counts, not wall time. Source strong-adversary extensions are outside this elementary proof scope.`,
viz(v){const t=v.slider('failure bound t',1,2,2,{int:true}),mask=v.slider('input mask',0,31,10,{int:true}),crash=v.select('initial crash',['none','P0'],'none'),policy=v.select('delivery order',['random','FIFO'],'random'),seed=v.slider('seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),budget=v.slider('delivery budget',5,1000,300,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'local round, phase, preference and decision'),g=panel(v,'32 seeded delivery counts',{axes:'box',xlabel:'run index',ylabel:'messages delivered'});let A,B;v.render(()=>{const c=crash.value==='none'?-1:0;A=benOr(5,t.value,mask.value,seed.value,budget.value,c,policy.value);B=benOrTrials(5,t.value,mask.value,seed.value,32,budget.value,c,policy.value);grid(s,A.P.map((p,i)=>[i===c?'failed':p.r,p.phase,p.pref,p.decision===null?'?':p.decision]));g.setView([-2,33],[-30,budget.value*1.15+30]);B.runs.forEach((x,i)=>g.dot(i,x.delivered,{r:3,color:x.complete?'green':'red'}));});v.legend([['green','all correct decided'],['red','budget-censored'],['purple','columns: round / phase / preference / decision']]);v.readout(()=>`Selected decisions ${A.P.map((p,i)=>i===0&&crash.value==='P0'?'failed':p.decision===null?'pending':p.decision).join(', ')}; delivery count ${A.delivered}. Trial completions ${B.success}/32, censored ${B.censored}; fraction ${f(B.rate)}, binomial MCSE ${f(B.mcse)} under the usual independent-repetition interpretation of separate PRNG seeds. Zero estimated MCSE at an endpoint does not certify probability0 or1.`);}
},
{
num:'5.11',kind:'Definition',name:'Failure detectors and Ω',
statement:String.raw`A failure detector supplies local outputs constrained by a specified relation to the crash pattern. The eventual-leader detector Ω outputs a process ID at each correct process; there exist an unknown finite time T and a correct process ℓ such that every output at every correct process after T equals ℓ. Earlier outputs may be wrong or disagree arbitrarily. Ω is an eventual leader oracle, not a list of suspected failures or a bounded-time crash diagnosis.`,
idea:String.raw`A silent process cannot be distinguished from a slow one using asynchronous messages alone. Ω adds a constraint on the infinite output tail, which eventually permits one correct proposer to make progress. Its definition does not reveal when stabilization has occurred: any observed finite prefix can be extended by later disagreements, so a finite agreement test cannot certify membership in Ω.`,
caption:String.raw`Compare a declared stabilizing oracle, a perpetually oscillating oracle and a tail that names a crashed leader. Every displayed entry is generated from the selected complete rule; the live tail checker reports only the visible suffix.`,
deps:['dist-5.7'],refs:['Lynch–Sastry2015 MIT-CSAIL-TR-2015-006 §5.3, printed p9'],links:{source:'https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Sastry/MIT-CSAIL-TR-2015-006.pdf'},tags:['failure detectors','eventual leader'],scope:String.raw`Four processes, P3 crashes initially, time indices0…23. In stabilizing mode the authored infinite rule chooses P0 at all correct processes from T onward; in wrong-tail mode it chooses failed P3. The checker examines only the finite displayed suffix; the complete rule, not finite observations, determines whether the Ω condition holds. No weakest-detector reduction is proved here.`,
viz(v){const mode=v.select('oracle rule',['stabilizes','oscillates','wrong tail'],'stabilizes'),gst=v.slider('declared tail starts T',0,20,8,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'oracle outputs over time');let A;v.render(()=>{A=omegaTrace(4,gst.value,mode.value);s.setView([-3.2,24],[-.7,3.8]);for(let p=0;p<4;p++){s.text(p===3?'P3×':'P'+p,-1.6,3-p,{size:10,align:'center'});if(p===3)continue;A.outputs.forEach((row,t)=>s.dot(t,3-p,{r:3.2,color:['blue','gold','green','red'][row[p]]}));}s.vline(gst.value,{color:'purple',dash:true});});v.legend([['blue','ID P0'],['gold','ID P1'],['green','ID P2'],['red','ID P3 (failed)'],['purple','declared tail boundary']]);v.readout(()=>`Correct processes P0,P1,P2. Visible suffix common-correct-leader check ${A.tailGood?'PASS':'FAIL'}. Complete authored rule ${mode.value==='stabilizes'?'satisfies Ω with leader P0':'does not satisfy Ω'}. The finite visible pass alone would not identify a stabilization time in an unknown execution.`);}
},
{
num:'5.12',kind:'Algorithm',name:'Consensus under partial synchrony',
statement:String.raw`Partial synchrony can mean unknown finite timing bounds, or known bounds that hold only after an unknown global stabilization time GST. With crash-stop failures and a responsive majority, consensus protocols can preserve agreement at all times and terminate once timing and leadership stabilize. In the ballot construction here, acceptors persist promises and accepted votes; a proposer gathers a majority's promises and carries forward the value of the highest accepted ballot reported before requesting majority acceptance.`,
idea:String.raw`Any two majority quorums intersect. Once a value is chosen, the highest-accepted rule inductively forces later successful ballots to preserve it. Safety therefore needs no timing bound. Progress additionally requires a correct eventual leader, a live responsive majority, and enough time for its prepare and accept exchanges before it starts a competing higher ballot. DLS proves partial-synchrony solvability; this widget executes a separately specified single-decree ballot construction from the MIT lecture.`,
caption:String.raw`Delay messages before GST and compare stable, adequately spaced leadership with perpetual competing ballots. Inspect each acceptor's promise and accepted value and the actual chosen-value log. A finite run with no chosen value is censored, not an impossibility result.`,
deps:['dist-5.11','dist-1.2'],see:['dist-7.5'],refs:['Dwork–Lynch–Stockmeyer1988 §§1,5.3–5.4, Theorems5.1–5.2; MIT6.852 lecture23 slides45–61 for displayed ballots'],links:{source:SRC.dls},tags:['partial synchrony','quorum intersection'],scope:String.raw`Authored five-acceptor protocol, majority3, P3 initially crashed, four responsive acceptors. Discrete messages before GST have seeded delays1…8; afterwards delay is1…4. Stable leader P0 starts new ballots every2d+1 ticks; unstable mode starts a higher ballot each tick. Persistent state has no crash recovery or Byzantine corruption. This is not a reproduction of DLS's clock-simulation protocol.`,
viz(v){const gst=v.slider('GST tick',0,12,3,{int:true}),delay=v.slider('post-GST one-way delay',1,4,2,{int:true}),mode=v.select('leadership',['stable after GST','perpetual competition'],'stable after GST'),seed=v.slider('delay seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),clock=v.slider('execution ticks',1,40,18,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'promises and accepted votes'),g=panel(v,'chosen ballots',{axes:'box',xlabel:'choice tick',ylabel:'chosen value'});let A;v.render(()=>{A=ballots(seed.value,gst.value,delay.value,clock.value,mode.value==='perpetual competition');const F=A.frames.at(-1);grid(s,F.acceptors.map((a,p)=>[p===3?'failed':a.promise,a.accepted?.ballot??'none',a.accepted?.value??'?']));g.setView([-2,42],[-.4,1.5]);A.chosen.forEach(c=>{g.dot(c.clock,c.value,{r:5,color:c.value?'gold':'blue'});});});v.legend([['blue','chosen0'],['gold','chosen1'],['purple','columns: promise / accepted ballot / value']]);v.readout(()=>`Chosen records ${A.chosen.map(c=>'b'+c.ballot+'→'+c.value+'@'+c.clock).join(', ')||'none in this prefix'}. Uniform chosen-value agreement ${A.agreement?'PASS':'FAIL'}. Attempts ${A.attempts.length}; quorum size3. Timing/leadership controls affect progress; majority intersection and persistent ballot rules protect safety.`);}
},
{
num:'5.13',kind:'Theorem',name:'Wait-free k-set agreement is impossible',
statement:String.raw`For n processes and 1≤k<n, no deterministic wait-free protocol using only atomic read/write shared registers solves k-set agreement for arbitrary inputs: each deciding value must be proposed, every correct process must decide after finitely many own steps regardless of other processes' speeds or crashes, and at most k different values may be decided. The obstruction needs a sufficiently rich input domain, in particular n distinct possible proposals; binary inputs make k≥2 trivial.`,
idea:String.raw`Saks and Zaharoglou encode schedules by a stronger weakly synchronous model and use the activity/boundary property imposed by proposal validity. Wait-freedom yields a finite refinement scale. Their triangulation graphs admit a parity argument: a coherent labeling has a fully labeled clique; the schedule construction makes that clique compatible with one long common prefix, forcing n distinct outcomes. The schedule-compatibility construction is essential; an arbitrary colored triangle is not automatically a protocol execution.`,
caption:String.raw`Compute a finite two-dimensional Sperner parity example. Boundary-valid colors produce an odd number of tricolored small triangles. Disable the boundary condition to see that the conclusion is no longer guaranteed. This geometric example is an analogy for the proof's parity step, not a generated read/write execution.`,
deps:['dist-1.4'],see:['dist-5.7','top-9.9'],refs:['Saks–Zaharoglou2000 Theorem1.1, §§3,5,6.1 (Lemmas6.1–6.4), pp1450–1451,1467–1471'],links:{source:SRC.kset},tags:['set agreement','topological obstruction'],scope:String.raw`The interactive triangle has authored seeded vertex labels, a regular mesh with m² triangles, and either coherent boundary labels or unconstrained labels. It verifies the finite two-dimensional parity statement only. The general theorem's advanced schedule graph construction in §6.2 is described rather than implemented. Planned TOP9.9 is a related link, not a written proof prerequisite.`,
viz(v){const mesh=v.slider('mesh subdivision m',1,8,4,{int:true}),seed=v.slider('label seed',1,100,1,{int:true}),mode=v.select('boundary labeling',['coherent','unconstrained'],'coherent'),s=panel(v,'finite Sperner parity example',{equal:true,x:[-.12,1.12],y:[-.12,1.02]});let A;v.render(()=>{A=sperner(mesh.value,seed.value,mode.value==='coherent');for(const t of A.triangles){const pts=t.map(i=>[A.vertices[i].x,A.vertices[i].y]);s.path([...pts,pts[0]],{color:new Set(t.map(i=>A.vertices[i].color)).size===3?'purple':'muted',width:new Set(t.map(i=>A.vertices[i].color)).size===3?2.5:1,alpha:.65});}for(const p of A.vertices)s.dot(p.x,p.y,{r:4,color:['blue','gold','green'][p.color]});});v.legend([['blue','label0'],['gold','label1'],['green','label2'],['purple','tricolored triangle']]);v.readout(()=>`Vertices ${A.vertices.length}; triangles ${A.triangles.length}; tricolored ${A.colorful.length}, parity ${A.colorful.length%2?'odd':'even'}. ${mode.value==='coherent'?'Boundary restriction holds; parity theorem requires odd.':'Boundary restriction disabled; no tricolor conclusion is guaranteed.'} These mesh vertices are not claimed to be process local states.`);}
},
{
num:'5.14',kind:'Algorithm',name:'Approximate agreement',
statement:String.raw`For synchronous complete communication with at most f Byzantine processes and n>3f, approximate agreement seeks outputs inside the convex hull of correct inputs with spread at most ε>0. For n=4,f=1, each correct process includes its own value, receives a value from every sender (fills omissions arbitrarily), discards the largest and smallest, and averages the remaining two. Repeating contracts the correct spread by at least a factor1/2 per round. Exact equality after a fixed finite number of rounds is not required.`,
idea:String.raw`The two retained endpoints lie in the old correct interval because at most one value is Byzantine. They also straddle the median of the three correct values. Their midpoint therefore lies between the midpoint of the old minimum and median and the midpoint of the median and old maximum. The new correct interval has length at most half the old one. After r rounds its spread is at most D0/2^r; a known upper bound on D0 gives a sufficient fixed-round deadline for a chosen ε.`,
caption:String.raw`Let one Byzantine sender report different real values to different correct receivers. Execute the actual trim-and-midpoint updates and compare the observed range with the analytic halving envelope. Inboxes and retained values can be inspected at the final round.`,
deps:['dist-5.1'],refs:['MIT6.852 lecture6 slides33–37; Dolev–Lynch–Pinter–Stark–Weihl1986 synchronous approximate agreement'],links:{source:SRC.mit},tags:['approximate agreement','Byzantine faults'],scope:String.raw`Three correct inputs0,2,6 and one faulty sender: reports a to P0, b to P1 and (a+b)/2 to P2 every round. Complete synchronous delivery, no omissions in this authored run. The proof applies to arbitrary Byzantine values each round for n4f1. The plot uses ordinary floating point and at most12 rounds; it does not certify tolerances below arithmetic resolution.`,
viz(v){const low=v.slider('Byzantine report a',-20,20,-5,{step:'any',decimals:2}),high=v.slider('Byzantine report b',-20,20,10,{step:'any',decimals:2}),round=v.slider('round count',0,12,4,{int:true}),s=panel(v,'correct values and halving envelope',{axes:'box',x:[-.5,12.5],y:[-.7,8],xlabel:'synchronous round',ylabel:'value'}),g=panel(v,'inputs / sorted inboxes');let A;v.render(()=>{A=approximate([0,2,6],round.value,low.value,high.value);for(let p=0;p<3;p++)s.path(A.frames.map((q,t)=>[t,q.values[p]]),{color:['blue','gold','green'][p],width:2});for(let t=0;t<A.frames.length;t++)s.path([[t,Math.min(...A.frames[t].values)],[t,Math.min(...A.frames[t].values)+6/2**t]],{color:'purple',alpha:.4});grid(g,A.frames.at(-1).rows.length?A.frames.at(-1).rows.map(row=>row.map(x=>f(x,2))):[[0],[2],[6]]);});v.legend([['blue','P0'],['gold','P1'],['green','P2'],['purple','D0/2^r above current minimum']]);v.readout(()=>`Requested reports a=${String(low.value)}, b=${String(high.value)}. Correct values ${A.values.map(x=>f(x,6)).join(', ')}; spread ${f(A.spread,8)}, analytic bound ${f(6/2**round.value,8)}. Convex-hull check ${A.values.every(x=>x>=0&&x<=6)?'PASS':'FAIL'}. Purple intervals start at each current correct minimum and have width D0/2^r; they enclose the actual correct range. ${round.value?'Retained inbox entries are columns1 and2 (zero-based).':'Initial inputs shown; no inbox update yet.'}`);}
},
{
num:'5.15',kind:'Paper',name:'Impossibility of Consensus with One Faulty Process',
introduces:['dist-5.7'],
paper:{authors:'Michael J. Fischer, Nancy A. Lynch and Michael S. Paterson',year:1985,title:'Impossibility of Distributed Consensus with One Faulty Process',venue:'Journal of the ACM32(2),374–382',url:SRC.flp,doi:'10.1145/3149.214121'},
statement:String.raw`Fischer, Lynch and Paterson proved that a completely asynchronous deterministic message protocol cannot always reach binary consensus when one process may stop. Reliable delivery and fair process scheduling do not rescue guaranteed termination. Their proof separates a finite commutativity lemma, a bivalent initial-state lemma, and a critical-event lemma from the final infinite fair-run construction.`,
idea:String.raw`In Lemma1, two finite schedules affecting disjoint sets of processes commute whenever both are applicable to the same configuration. Local states and the multiset of pending messages match after either order. This permits the critical-event proof to compare indistinguishable extensions. The final construction repeatedly schedules old pending events and rotates processes while staying bivalent, rather than violating fairness by permanently withholding a fixed message.`,
caption:String.raw`Compare the two orders of actual local append-and-send events. Distinct receivers yield equal final local states and equal message multisets; using the same receiver changes the local order and invalidates the disjoint-process premise. This is a concrete instance of the lemma, not the whole impossibility proof.`,
deps:['dist-5.1','dist-1.2','dist-1.7'],refs:['Fischer–Lynch–Paterson1985 pp374–380, especially Lemma1 and Theorem1'],links:{source:SRC.flp},tags:['FLP','primary paper','commutativity'],scope:String.raw`Authored two-event transition system: each event appends its bit to the receiver's local string and emits the resulting string as a message; pending messages are compared as sorted multisets. Original FLP models arbitrary deterministic local transition functions and receives that may return null. These two toy events illustrate only the disjoint-process commutation condition.`,
viz(v){const mode=v.select('event receivers',['distinct processes','same process'],'distinct processes'),s=panel(v,'commuting-event diamond',{x:[-.25,1.25],y:[-.25,1.25]});let A;v.render(()=>{A=diamond(mode.value==='same process');const vertices=[[.5,1],[0,.5],[1,.5],[.5,0]];for(const[a,b]of[[0,1],[0,2],[1,3],[2,3]])s.path([vertices[a],vertices[b]],{color:A.equal?'green':'red',width:2});s.text('C',.5,1.08,{align:'center'});s.text('e then f',0,.65,{align:'center',size:11});s.text('f then e',1,.65,{align:'center',size:11});s.text(A.equal?'same configuration':'different configurations',.5,-.1,{align:'center',size:12,color:A.equal?'green':'red'});});v.legend([['green','disjoint events commute'],['red','same receiver: premise fails']]);v.readout(()=>`e targets P${A.a.p}, f targets P${A.b.p}. e;f local strings ${JSON.stringify(A.ab.local)}, buffer ${JSON.stringify(A.ab.buffer)}. f;e local strings ${JSON.stringify(A.ba.local)}, buffer ${JSON.stringify(A.ba.buffer)}. Exact configuration equality ${A.equal}. No infinite schedule is generated or certified here.`);}
}
]};
